{"type":"exercise","type_name":"Exercise","number":"","title":"A2: Drill","title_html":"A2: Drill","html":"<p>Each part takes a line or two. The parts run from writing a counting\nsentence, through naming the theorem that answers a question, to judging\nwhat a theorem does and does not say.</p>\n<div class=\"enumerate\" data-options=\"label=(\\alph*)\">\n<ol type=\"1\">\n<li>Write <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"\\sigma_2\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-msub><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D70E TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-script style=\"vertical-align: -0.15em;\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" size=\"s\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c32\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-script></mjx-msub></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span>, “there are at\nleast two elements”.</li>\n<li>Write “there are at most two elements”.</li>\n<li>Which theorem says “every consistent theory has a model”?</li>\n<li>Which says “if every finite part of <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"T\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D447 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> has a model, <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"T\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D447 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>T</mi></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> has one”?</li>\n<li>True or false: completeness says that every sentence true in <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"(\\mathbb{N}; 0, S, +, \\cdot, <)\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c28\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-TeXAtom texclass=\"ORD\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-ds mjx-b\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2115 TEX-A\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-TeXAtom><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c3B\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"2\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-i\" space=\"2\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c1D446 TEX-I\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"2\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2B\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"2\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c22C5\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"2\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c3C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c29\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">N</mi></mrow><mo>;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> is derivable\nwith no premises.</li>\n<li>True or false: a theory with models of every finite size has an\ninfinite model.</li>\n<li>Is “the domain is finite” expressible by one sentence?</li>\n<li>True or false: in a model of all sentences true in <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"\\mathbb{N}\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-TeXAtom texclass=\"ORD\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-ds mjx-b\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2115 TEX-A\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-TeXAtom></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">N</mi></mrow></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span>, every element is the value of a\nnumeral.</li>\n<li>Over what does the second-order induction principle quantify that\nthe schema does not?</li>\n<li>Is derivability of a propositional claim decidable? Of a monadic\nfirst-order sentence (one-place predicate symbols only; no constants,\nfunction symbols or <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"=\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c3D\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span>)? Of a\nfirst-order sentence in general?</li>\n<li>Does <span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"(\\mathbb{R}; 0, 1, +, \\cdot,\n<)\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c28\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-TeXAtom texclass=\"ORD\"><mjx-mi class=\"mjx-ds mjx-b\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c211D TEX-A\"></mjx-c></mjx-mi></mjx-TeXAtom><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c3B\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"2\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c30\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"2\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c31\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"2\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2B\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"2\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c22C5\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c2C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\" space=\"2\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c3C\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo><mjx-mo class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c29\"></mjx-c></mjx-mo></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi></mrow><mo>;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>+</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> have a model of the same sentences whose domain can be\nlisted?</li>\n<li>Why is a theory whose models are exactly the structures of size\n<span class=\"math inline\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax\" jax=\"CHTML\" style=\"font-size: 113.1%; position: relative;\" data-tex=\"3\"><mjx-math class=\" MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c33\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container></span> possible, and one whose models\nare exactly the finite structures not?</li>\n</ol>\n</div>","file":"ch07-models-and-theories/06-limits-of-first-order-logic.html"}